"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[33106],{333106:e=>{e.exports='# Anti-Patterns on Shared Compute\n\nThese patterns describe subtle behavioral differences between Spark Classic and Spark\nConnect that do not raise errors but produce silently wrong results or severe performance\ndegradation. They are not API incompatibilities (the code runs) but correctness or\nperformance traps that require code changes when migrating to Standard/Serverless.\n\n## Anti-Pattern: Overwriting temp views (stale DataFrame references)\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# Pattern 1: Function that reuses the same temp view name\ndef create_temp_view_and_create_dataframe(x):\n    spark.range(x).createOrReplaceTempView("tempview")\n    return spark.sql("SELECT * FROM tempview")\n\ndf10 = create_temp_view_and_create_dataframe(10)\ndf100 = create_temp_view_and_create_dataframe(100)\ndf10.collect()   # Unexpected: returns 100 rows — resolves to the latest view, not the one at creation time\ndf100.collect()  # returns 100 rows\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 2: Loop or pipeline that overwrites the same temp view\nfor step in ["raw", "cleaned", "enriched"]:\n    process(step).createOrReplaceTempView("staging")\n    results[step] = spark.sql("SELECT * FROM staging")\n# All three DataFrames point to the last "enriched" view\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 3: spark.table() with reused name\ndf_v1 = spark.table("my_temp_view")\nspark.range(999).createOrReplaceTempView("my_temp_view")\ndf_v1.collect()  # Unexpected: returns 999 rows — resolves to the latest view at action time\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nIn Spark Connect, the DataFrame plan references the temp view **by name only** (an\nunresolved `named_table` proto node). **Overwriting a temp view does NOT update\npreviously-created DataFrame variables** — they still hold a reference to the\n**previous lazy plan**, which stores only the unresolved view name. Spark Connect\ndefers all plan analysis until an action fires (see [3]), so those DataFrame variables\nresolve to whichever view definition is current at action time — not the one at\ncreation time. In Spark Classic, eager analysis embeds the view\'s concrete logical\nplan at creation time, making DataFrames immune to later replacements.\n\n### How to Convert\n\n**Preferred: Refactor to pass DataFrames directly instead of round-tripping through\ntemp views.** This eliminates the stale-reference problem entirely.\n\nBefore (anti-pattern):\n\n```python\ndef process(x):\n    spark.range(x).createOrReplaceTempView("tempview")\n    return spark.sql("SELECT * FROM tempview")\n```\n\nAfter (preferred - pass DataFrames directly):\n\n```python\ndef process(x):\n    return spark.range(x)\n\ndf10 = process(10)\ndf100 = process(100)\n```\n\n**If SQL is needed, use CTEs in `spark.sql()` instead of temp views:**\n\n```python\nresult = spark.sql("""\n    WITH staged AS (SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE status = \'active\')\n    SELECT user_id, count(*) FROM staged GROUP BY user_id\n""")\n```\n\n**If temp views are required (e.g., for SQL interop or ai_query), use UUID-based\nunique view names to prevent overwrites:**\n\n```python\nimport uuid\nview_name = f"`tempview_{uuid.uuid4()}`"\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(view_name)\nresult = spark.sql(f"SELECT * FROM {view_name}")\n```\n\nFor loops/pipelines using the UUID fallback, generate a new name per iteration:\n\n```python\nimport uuid\nfor step in ["raw", "cleaned", "enriched"]:\n    view_name = f"`staging_{step}_{uuid.uuid4()}`"\n    process(step).createOrReplaceTempView(view_name)\n    results[step] = spark.sql(f"SELECT * FROM {view_name}")\n```\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative (full example)\n\n```python\n# Option A (preferred): pass DataFrames directly\ndef create_dataframe(x):\n    return spark.range(x)\n\ndf10 = create_dataframe(10)\ndf100 = create_dataframe(100)\nassert len(df10.collect()) == 10    # Correct: independent DataFrames\nassert len(df100.collect()) == 100\n\n# Option B: if temp views are needed, use unique names per call\nimport uuid\n\ndef create_via_temp_view(x):\n    view_name = f"`tempview_{uuid.uuid4()}`"\n    spark.range(x).createOrReplaceTempView(view_name)\n    return spark.sql(f"SELECT * FROM {view_name}")\n\ndf10 = create_via_temp_view(10)\ndf100 = create_via_temp_view(100)\nassert len(df10.collect()) == 10    # Correct: each DF has its own view\nassert len(df100.collect()) == 100\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- The `spark.table("name")` API has the same stale-reference behavior as\n  `spark.sql("SELECT * FROM name")`.\n- If temp views are only used once (never replaced), this pattern is safe.\n\n---\n\n## Anti-Pattern: UDF closure over mutable external variables\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# Pattern 1: UDF captures a variable that is modified later\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import udf\n\nx = 123\n\n@udf("INT")\ndef foo():\n    return x\n\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([(1,)], ["dummy"])\ndf = df.select(foo())\nx = 456\ndf.show()  # BUG: prints 456, not 123\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 2: UDF defined inside a loop captures the loop variable\nimport json\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import udf, col\n\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [{"values": \'{"column_1": 1, "column_2": 2}\'}], ["values"]\n)\nfor j in [\'column_1\', \'column_2\']:\n    def extract_value(col_val):\n        return json.loads(col_val).get(j)\n    extract_value_udf = udf(extract_value)\n    df = df.withColumn(j, extract_value_udf(col(\'values\')))\ndf.show()  # BUG: shows 2 for BOTH columns (last value of j)\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 3: @udf decorator with external config/threshold\nthreshold = 100\n\n@udf("BOOLEAN")\ndef is_above_threshold(val):\n    return val > threshold\n\ndf = df.withColumn("flag", is_above_threshold(col("amount")))\nthreshold = 0  # Changed before action\ndf.show()      # BUG: uses threshold=0, not 100\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nSpark Connect defers UDF serialization until an action fires (see [3]). The UDF\ncaptures variables **by reference**, so any changes between definition and execution\nare picked up at action time. In Spark Classic, UDFs are eagerly serialized at\ndefinition time, freezing captured values immediately.\n\n### How to Convert\n\n**Step 1: Identify any UDF that references a variable defined outside the UDF body\n(module-level variables, loop variables, config values, etc.).**\n\nLook for these patterns:\n\n- `@udf` decorated function referencing outer variables\n- `udf(lambda ...)` or `udf(some_function)` where the function reads outer scope\n- UDF defined inside a `for` loop\n\n**Step 2: Wrap the UDF in a function factory that takes the external value as a\nparameter. The factory returns a new UDF with the value frozen.**\n\nBefore (variable captured by reference):\n\n```python\n@udf("INT")\ndef foo():\n    return x  # x captured by reference\n```\n\nAfter (value frozen via factory):\n\n```python\ndef make_foo(captured_x):\n    @udf("INT")\n    def foo():\n        return captured_x  # captured_x is frozen at call time\n    return foo\n\nmy_udf = make_foo(x)  # freeze the current value of x\n```\n\n**Step 3: For loop variables, call the factory inside the loop.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\nfor j in [\'column_1\', \'column_2\']:\n    def extract_value(col_val):\n        return json.loads(col_val).get(j)\n    extract_value_udf = udf(extract_value)\n    df = df.withColumn(j, extract_value_udf(col(\'values\')))\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\ndef make_extract_value_udf(field):\n    def extract_value(col_val):\n        return json.loads(col_val).get(field)\n    return udf(extract_value)\n\nfor j in [\'column_1\', \'column_2\']:\n    extract_value_udf = make_extract_value_udf(j)  # freezes j\n    df = df.withColumn(j, extract_value_udf(col(\'values\')))\n```\n\n**Step 4: For config/threshold values, freeze them at UDF creation.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\nthreshold = 100\n@udf("BOOLEAN")\ndef is_above(val):\n    return val > threshold\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\ndef make_threshold_udf(thresh):\n    @udf("BOOLEAN")\n    def is_above(val):\n        return val > thresh\n    return is_above\n\nis_above_udf = make_threshold_udf(threshold)\ndf = df.withColumn("flag", is_above_udf(col("amount")))\n```\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative (full example)\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import udf, col\nimport json\n\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [{"values": \'{"column_1": 1, "column_2": 2}\'}], ["values"]\n)\n\n# Factory function: takes the field name, returns a UDF with that value frozen\ndef make_extract_value_udf(field):\n    def extract_value(val):\n        return json.loads(val).get(field)\n    return udf(extract_value)\n\nfor j in [\'column_1\', \'column_2\']:\n    extract_value_udf = make_extract_value_udf(j)\n    df = df.withColumn(j, extract_value_udf(col(\'values\')))\n\ndf.show()  # Correctly shows 1 for column_1, 2 for column_2\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- The key transformation is: **move the `@udf` / `udf()` call inside a factory\n  function, pass external values as factory arguments**.\n- The factory creates a new Python scope where the value is bound as a local\n  parameter, preventing late-binding surprises.\n\n---\n\n## Anti-Pattern: Error handling relying on eager schema analysis\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# Pattern 1: try/except around transformations expecting immediate errors\ntry:\n    df = df.select("name", "age")\n    df = df.withColumn(\n        "age_group",\n        when(col("age") < 18, "minor").otherwise("adult")\n    )\n    df = df.filter(col("age_with_typo") > 6)\n    # ^ "age_with_typo" does NOT exist, but no error is raised here\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f"Error: {repr(e)}")\n# The except block is never entered — the typo silently passes\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 2: Validation function that checks transformations are valid\ndef validate_query(df, transforms):\n    try:\n        for t in transforms:\n            df = t(df)\n        return True  # Assumes all columns resolved correctly\n    except AnalysisException:\n        return False\n# On Connect, always returns True even with bad column references\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 3: Error reporting deferred to much later in the pipeline\ndf = spark.read.table("events")\ndf = df.filter(col("timestampp") > "2024-01-01")  # Typo: no error\ndf = df.groupBy("user_id").count()                  # Still no error\n# ... 50 more lines of transformations ...\ndf.write.saveAsTable("output")  # Error finally surfaces here\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nSpark Connect defers schema analysis until an action or schema access fires (see [3]).\nTransformations build a local unresolved proto plan — no column reference validation\noccurs until `collect()`, `show()`, `count()`, `write`, `df.columns`, or `df.schema`.\nIn Spark Classic, schema analysis is eager — each transformation validates immediately.\n\n### How to Convert\n\n**Step 1: Find try/except blocks that wrap only transformations (no actions) and\nexpect to catch `AnalysisException` or column-resolution errors.**\n\n**Step 2: Add `df.columns` (or `df.schema`) as the last line inside the try block\nto force the server to analyze the plan and surface errors.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    df = df.select("name", "age")\n    df = df.filter(col("age_with_typo") > 6)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f"Error: {repr(e)}")\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    df = df.select("name", "age")\n    df = df.filter(col("age_with_typo") > 6)\n    df.columns  # Forces Analyze RPC — surfaces the error here\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f"Error: {repr(e)}")\n```\n\n**Step 3: For validation functions, add a schema access call before returning.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\ndef validate_query(df, transforms):\n    try:\n        for t in transforms:\n            df = t(df)\n        return True\n    except AnalysisException:\n        return False\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\ndef validate_query(df, transforms):\n    try:\n        for t in transforms:\n            df = t(df)\n        df.schema  # Trigger analysis to validate all columns\n        return True\n    except AnalysisException:\n        return False\n```\n\n**Step 4: For long pipelines, insert periodic `df.columns` checkpoints to catch\nerrors closer to their source rather than at the final action.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.read.table("events")\ndf = df.filter(col("timestampp") > "2024-01-01")  # Typo\ndf = df.groupBy("user_id").count()\n# ... many more transformations ...\ndf.write.saveAsTable("output")  # Error surfaces very late\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.read.table("events")\ndf = df.filter(col("timestampp") > "2024-01-01")\ndf.columns  # Checkpoint: catch the typo here, close to the source\ndf = df.groupBy("user_id").count()\n# ... many more transformations ...\ndf.write.saveAsTable("output")\n```\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative (full example)\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import col, when\n\ntry:\n    df = df.select("name", "age")\n    df = df.withColumn(\n        "age_group",\n        when(col("age") < 18, "minor").otherwise("adult")\n    )\n    df = df.filter(col("age_with_typo") > 6)\n    # Add this line to force eager analysis:\n    df.columns\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f"Error: {repr(e)}")\n    # Now correctly catches: AnalysisException: Column \'age_with_typo\' does not exist\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- Use `df.columns`, `df.schema`, or `df.printSchema()` to force analysis and surface\n  errors early. All three trigger an Analyze RPC (see "Excessive Analyze RPC calls"\n  anti-pattern for cost implications).\n- This is a fundamental behavioral difference, not a bug.\n\n---\n\n## Anti-Pattern: Excessive Analyze RPC calls in loops (df.columns / df.schema)\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# Pattern 1: df.columns inside a loop (200 Analyze RPCs)\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as F\n\ndf = spark.range(10)\nfor i in range(200):\n    new_column_name = str(i)\n    if new_column_name not in df.columns:\n        # ^ df.columns triggers a new Analyze RPC in EVERY iteration\n        df = df.withColumn(new_column_name, F.col("id") + i)\ndf.show()\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 2: df.schema inside a comprehension (O(N) RPCs)\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType\n\nstruct_column_fields = {\n    col_schema.name: df.select(col_schema.name + ".*").columns\n    #                                                  ^^^^^^^^\n    # Each .columns call creates a new DF and triggers Analyze RPC\n    for col_schema in df.schema\n    if isinstance(col_schema.dataType, StructType)\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 3: Repeated df.columns checks in conditional logic\nfor col_name in candidate_columns:\n    if col_name in df.columns:        # RPC\n        df = df.withColumn(col_name + "_clean", clean_udf(col(col_name)))\n    if col_name + "_raw" in df.columns:  # Another RPC\n        df = df.drop(col_name + "_raw")\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nIn Spark Connect, `df.columns` and `df.schema` send an Analyze RPC to the server the\n**first time** they are called on a given DataFrame client object. The result is cached\non that object — calling `.columns` twice on the **same** variable only sends one RPC.\nThe issue arises when the loop **reassigns the variable** (e.g.\n`df = df.withColumn(...)` or `df_flat = df_flat.select(...)`): each reassignment\ncreates a new client object with an empty cache, so the next `.columns` call pays\na fresh RPC. On Spark Classic, `.columns` reads from the eagerly resolved local schema\nwith zero network cost.\n\nThis is a common cause of severe performance degradation when migrating to Standard\ncompute.\n\n### How to Convert\n\n**Step 1: Find any `df.columns` or `df.schema` call inside a loop, comprehension,\nor repeated conditional block.**\n\n**Step 2: Hoist the call above the loop — call `df.columns` once before the loop and\ncache the result in a local Python set. Update the set manually as you add/drop\ncolumns.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\nfor i in range(200):\n    if str(i) not in df.columns:  # RPC each iteration\n        df = df.withColumn(str(i), F.col("id") + i)\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\ncolumns = set(df.columns)  # Single RPC call, cached\nfor i in range(200):\n    name = str(i)\n    if name not in columns:       # Local set lookup, free\n        df = df.withColumn(name, F.col("id") + i)\n        columns.add(name)         # Keep local cache in sync\n```\n\n**Step 3: For schema inspection inside comprehensions, extract field info from the\nalready-fetched schema object rather than creating intermediate DataFrames.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\nstruct_column_fields = {\n    col_schema.name: df.select(col_schema.name + ".*").columns  # RPC per column\n    for col_schema in df.schema\n    if isinstance(col_schema.dataType, StructType)\n}\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\nschema = df.schema  # Single RPC call\nstruct_column_fields = {\n    col_schema.name: [f.name for f in col_schema.dataType.fields]  # Zero RPCs\n    for col_schema in schema\n    if isinstance(col_schema.dataType, StructType)\n}\n```\n\n**Step 4: For multiple `df.columns` checks in conditional blocks, cache once and\nreuse.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\nfor col_name in candidate_columns:\n    if col_name in df.columns:              # RPC\n        df = df.withColumn(...)\n    if col_name + "_raw" in df.columns:     # Another RPC\n        df = df.drop(col_name + "_raw")\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\ncurrent_cols = set(df.columns)              # Single RPC\nfor col_name in candidate_columns:\n    if col_name in current_cols:            # Local lookup\n        df = df.withColumn(...)\n        current_cols.add(col_name + "_clean")\n    if col_name + "_raw" in current_cols:   # Local lookup\n        df = df.drop(col_name + "_raw")\n        current_cols.discard(col_name + "_raw")\n```\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative (full example)\n\n```python\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as F\n\ndf = spark.range(10)\ncolumns = set(df.columns)  # Cache column names - one Analyze RPC\nall_columns = [df["*"]]\nfor i in range(200):\n    new_column_name = str(i)\n    if new_column_name not in columns:\n        all_columns.append((F.col("id") + 1).alias(new_column_name))\n        columns.add(new_column_name)\ndf = df.select(*all_columns)\ndf.show()\n```\n\n**Step 5: For loops that reassign the DataFrame AND need to inspect columns from newly\nexpanded struct fields, read `df.schema` once before the loop and derive sub-field\nnames from the `StructType` object — never call `.columns` on the post-select DataFrame.**\n\nBefore:\n\n```python\ndf_flat = df\nfor _ in range(50):\n    if "item" not in df_flat.columns:   # RPC each iteration (new df each time)\n        break\n    keep = [c for c in df_flat.columns if c != "item"]\n    df_flat = df_flat.select(*keep, "item.*")\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType\n\ncol_types = {f.name: f.dataType for f in df.schema.fields}  # Single RPC\ndf_flat = df\nfor _ in range(50):\n    if "item" not in col_types:\n        break\n    item_type = col_types["item"]\n    if not isinstance(item_type, StructType):\n        break\n    keep = {k: v for k, v in col_types.items() if k != "item"}\n    col_types = {**keep, **{f.name: f.dataType for f in item_type.fields}}\n    df_flat = df_flat.select(*keep, "item.*")\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- **Rule of thumb:** call `df.columns` or `df.schema` at most once per DataFrame\n  in a code block; store the result in a local variable.\n- Calling `.columns` multiple times on the **same** DataFrame variable is safe — only\n  the first call sends an RPC; subsequent calls return the cached result.\n- If columns are added/dropped in a loop, maintain a parallel `set()` or `dict()`.\n- `df.printSchema()` also triggers an Analyze RPC — avoid in loops.\n- Cached column names may become stale if the underlying table is modified\n  concurrently. Re-read schema at logical boundaries in long-running workflows.\n'}}]);
